## Why This Is Asked
New angle on complexity-analysis: maintaining LIS under online batched updates tests block-based design, amortized reasoning, and practical trade-offs beyond typical path/graph problems.

## Key Concepts
- Longest Increasing Subsequence (LIS) dynamics
- sqrt-decomposition / block partitioning
- per-block LIS profiles and cross-block composition
- amortized cost vs full recomputation
- practical optimizations and memory bounds

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple block LIS recompute (tails-based)
function computeBlockLIS(block) {
  const tails = [];
  for (const x of block) {
    let i = 0, j = tails.length;
    while (i < j) {
      const m = (i + j) >> 1;
      if (tails[m] < x) i = m + 1; else j = m;
    }
    tails[i] = x;
  }
  return tails;
}

// Placeholder for merging block profiles into global LIS length
function mergeBlocks(blocks) {
  let tails = [];
  for (const b of blocks) {
    // apply b.profile to current tails (conceptual)
    tails = applyProfile(tails, b.profile);
  }
  return tails.length;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for non-increasing or multi-criteria LIS (e.g., by value and position)?
- What would be the impact of choosing K adaptively based on B and n?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about streaming similarity with limited memory, a practical beginner-level challenge that still touches core complexity trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- MinHash sketches for Jaccard similarity
- Batch processing costs vs per-element costs
- Trade-offs: d (accuracy) vs memory, impact of collisions
- Exact vs approximate counting implications

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: initialize sketches for A and B
class MinHash {
  constructor(d, maxHash) { this.d = d; this.sig = Array(d).fill(Infinity); this.maxHash = maxHash; }
  add(x) { for (let i=0; i<this.d; i++) { const h = hash_i(x, i) % this.maxHash; if (h < this.sig[i]) this.sig[i] = h; } }
  resembles(other) { let matches = 0; for (let i=0; i<this.d; i++) if (this.sig[i] === other.sig[i]) matches++; return matches / this.d; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you choose d given target accuracy ε?
- How handle skewed data or duplicates in batches?
- How would you extend to weighted or multi-set similarity?

